Claim nothing about blocking that the standard library will not support.

The mixer is annotated [[clang::nonblocking]] and the project turns the
check into an error of its own accord: -Werror=function-effects is on its
own command line. Clang then verifies the claim through every call, and
against libstdc++ it fails --

    alc/alu.cpp:262:9: error: function with 'nonblocking' attribute must not
    call non-'nonblocking' function 'std::variant<...>::...'

-- because libstdc++'s std::variant throws and holds function-local statics,
neither of which clang can see past. libc++ is the standard library this
annotation was written against, and there it holds.

So the attribute is applied when compiling against libc++ and not
otherwise. <memory> and <type_traits> are included above this line, so
_LIBCPP_VERSION is defined by the time it is read if it is going to be.
Nothing about the mixer changes; what changes is whether a claim about it is
made to a compiler that cannot check it here.

--- a/common/opthelpers.h
+++ b/common/opthelpers.h
@@ -62,7 +62,7 @@
 #define LIFETIMEBOUND
 #endif
 
-#if HAS_ATTRIBUTE(clang::nonblocking)
+#if HAS_ATTRIBUTE(clang::nonblocking) && defined(_LIBCPP_VERSION)
 #define NONBLOCKING [[clang::nonblocking]]
 #else
 #define NONBLOCKING
